(Regression test for bug #44: a list or map interpolated into a format
 string rendered as text only in `Print` position. Every other sink handed
 the collection's heap POINTER to the integer formatter, so the program
 printed an address - a different one on every run, which also made any
 generated program that did this look nondeterministic.

 LANGUAGE.md's "Format Strings as Values" says a format string used as a
 value materializes into a fresh string, and "Format Strings Everywhere"
 says every string-taking statement accepts one and all sinks render
 identically. A text initializer is the plainest of those sinks.

 Acceptance: each pair of lines below is identical - the Print control
 first, then the same interpolation captured into a text.)

a list called flat is [1, 2, 3].
a list called nested is [[1, 2], [3]].
a list called 'the empty list' is [].
a list called assorted is [1, "two", 3.5].
a map called person is {"name": "Ada", "born": 1815}.
a map called 'the empty map' is {}.

Print "{flat}".
a text called 'the flat list' is "{flat}".
Print 'the flat list'.

Print "{nested}".
a text called 'the nested list' is "{nested}".
Print 'the nested list'.

Print "{'the empty list'}".
a text called 'the empty rendering' is "{'the empty list'}".
Print 'the empty rendering'.

Print "{assorted}".
a text called 'the assorted list' is "{assorted}".
Print 'the assorted list'.

Print "{person}".
a text called 'the person' is "{person}".
Print 'the person'.

Print "{'the empty map'}".
a text called 'the empty map rendering' is "{'the empty map'}".
Print 'the empty map rendering'.

(A collection is not the whole string - the literal text around it must
 still land in the right place.)
Print "before {flat} after".
a text called surrounded is "before {flat} after".
Print surrounded.
